<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  fleet-watch — merge-is-ignition. Polls allowlisted, branch-protected repos for
  `status: ready` plans on main with no work PR, and spawns one orchestrator each.

  RESIDENT (SPEC.md Revisions, 2026-09-10 "a scheduler may be resident"): this
  unit passes the resident flag and cycles internally rather than exiting each
  time, so `KeepAlive` (not `StartInterval`) is what keeps it running. That is not an
  implementation detail here: a `StartInterval` job exits every cycle and
  reparents every orchestrator it spawned to launchd as an ORPHAN, and an
  orphan cannot open a local-network connection from a newly-launched binary
  (six of six autonomous dispatches died at their first `git fetch` under that
  shape). It also SELF-RESTARTS (plan 0040, SPEC.md Revisions 2026-09-11): once
  its own pin advances to a clean, reviewed commit and nothing it launched is
  still running, it ends its loop between cycles and `KeepAlive` relaunches it
  on the merged code — nobody needs to run `launchctl kickstart` by hand for
  this unit to pick up a merged change to itself.

  Install (opshost only — the ledger host, and the 2026-09-01 decision to run as the
  logged-in user, not root: a root daemon makes every lease and lockfile
  root:wheel, and the user's own fleet-status/prepush/release then hit
  PermissionError and skip with a warning).
    mkdir -p ~/dev/.fleet
    cp launchd/org.eunomia.fleet-watch.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    # fill FLEET_WATCH_REPOS, FLEET_NTFY_URL, FLEET_OPERATOR_UID and
    # FLEET_IMPLEMENTER_UIDS first — see below
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.eunomia.fleet-watch.plist
  Reload after editing (a frozen job needs bootout+bootstrap, not kickstart):
    launchctl bootout gui/$(id -u)/org.eunomia.fleet-watch
    launchctl bootstrap gui/$(id -u) ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.eunomia.fleet-watch.plist

  RUNS FROM THE PINNED WORKTREE, not ~/dev/eunomia — the same rule fleet-collect
  states: the shared clone was observed on three different branches in one
  afternoon, and a scheduled job is the worst place for that because nobody is
  watching when it fires. This unit's own pin is checked by
  org.eunomia.fleet-pin-watch, which checks that same worktree — so a fix to
  this file does not reach this unit until someone re-points, and until then
  pin-watch says so.

  ENVIRONMENT, and the two mistakes worth naming.

  FLEET_WATCH_REPOS is the allowlist and there is no default: an unconfigured
  watcher reads nothing at all. `config/repos.conf` is NOT consulted — plan 0013's
  main-via-contents-API read is unbuilt, and `fleet-repo` says so to the operator's
  face. Enrolment there is a record, not a dispatch.

  Do NOT set FLEET_ADMIN_TOKEN_CMD. It is retired. The branch-protection verdict
  now comes from keyvault's forgejo-broker (infra ADR-0003 §2) — the watcher asks a
  question and receives an answer, holding nothing it could leak. Reintroducing an
  admin-token recipe here would hand a scheduled process the ability to PATCH a
  protection rule, which is precisely the ignition forgery this whole mechanism
  exists to prevent.

  FLEET_BROKER_SOCKET must actually reach the broker — org.eunomia.fleet-broker-tunnel
  provides it. With no verdict the watcher pages once and scans NOTHING — it does
  not refuse each repo, because an unreachable broker says nothing about any repo,
  and collapsing UNDETERMINED into refusal is the Watcher blind misdiagnosis with
  the alarm inverted. That is fail-closed and correct; it is also indistinguishable
  from "nothing to do" if you are not reading watch.log.

  FLEET_OPERATOR_UID gates the `respawn` comment path, bound to a numeric id so a
  rename cannot grant it. Unset = respawn disabled, which is a safe default.
  Find it with:
    curl -sS -H @<(printf 'Authorization: token '; ~/bin/fetch-forgejo-token.sh) \
      "http://forge.example:3000/api/v1/users/operator" | python3 -c 'import json,sys;print(json.load(sys.stdin)["id"])'
  The URL is written out because FLEET_FORGEJO_URL is set BELOW, inside this file:
  it is not in the shell you paste that line into.

  Cadence: 120s between cycles (`watch_interval()`'s default), not a launchd
  interval — resident mode holds the process's own cycle lock for its whole
  lifetime, which is a STRONGER singleton than a `StartInterval` job re-taking
  the lock each time: there is no window between cycles for a second scheduler
  to slip in.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>org.eunomia.fleet-watch</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/python3</string>
    <string>/Users/operator/.local/share/pins/eunomia/bin/fleet-watch</string>
    <string>--resident</string>
  </array>

  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <!-- no default: an unconfigured watcher scans nothing. comma-separated owner/repo -->
    <key>FLEET_WATCH_REPOS</key><string></string>

    <!-- replace with the real Fleet-ops push URL at install; empty = print-only,
         and the runbook treats pushes as THE failure channel -->
    <key>FLEET_NTFY_URL</key><string></string>

    <!-- numeric Forgejo user id; empty disables the respawn comment path -->
    <key>FLEET_OPERATOR_UID</key><string></string>

    <!-- numeric ids of the implementer accounts. Dedupe gates SPEND, and the
         login fallback (`implbot`) is not rename-proof: a rename makes every
         historically marked PR invisible and re-dispatches every merged plan.
         Find them the same way as FLEET_OPERATOR_UID above. -->
    <key>FLEET_IMPLEMENTER_UIDS</key><string></string>

    <key>FLEET_FORGEJO_URL</key><string>http://forge.example:3000</string>

    <!-- the ssh forward from org.eunomia.fleet-broker-tunnel lands here -->
    <key>FLEET_BROKER_SOCKET</key><string>/Users/operator/run/forgejo-broker.sock</string>

    <!-- FLEET_PINS is ABSENT ON PURPOSE; org.eunomia.fleet-pin-watch owns the
         pin check. Setting it here would check the same pins every 120s rather
         than every 1800s, INSIDE the cycle lock and across two network fetches:
         the exact cost that pin-watch's pins-only mode was moved out of the
         lock to avoid, re-entered from the other side. It would also give both
         units RunAtLoad plus a shared state file, so at login two `git fetch`
         race in the same worktree and one transition pages twice or more. -->
  </dict>

  <!-- No StartInterval: resident mode cycles internally (watch_interval(),
       default 120s) instead of exiting each time. KeepAlive is what relaunches
       it, on a crash and on the self-restart plan 0040 added: the watcher ends
       its own loop once it sees its own pin has moved to a clean, reviewed
       commit, and this is what picks it back up on the merged code. -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!-- NO ProcessType, LowPriorityIO or Nice, deliberately, and this unit is the
       one place in launchd/ where that is the right call. The sibling watchers
       keep them; this one SPAWNS.

       All three are inherited by the orchestrators spawn() starts, and an
       unprivileged child cannot undo any of them: measured on opshost, a real
       launchd job with ProcessType Background reports darwinbg 0 and sits at
       priority 4, so the clamp is the process TYPE rather than the darwinbg
       flag, and setpriority cannot clear it; nice cannot be lowered by an
       unprivileged process at all. Once part 2 wires the hop, every implementer
       session dispatched from here would run in the background band and
       I/O-throttled, invisibly, because `nice` still reports 0.

       What the keys were bought for was "polling a forge must never delay a
       login". The poll is one HTTP round per 120s against a LAN forge; it was
       never the expensive thing here. The expensive thing is the child, and the
       child must not be throttled. -->

  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/operator/dev/.fleet/watch.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/operator/dev/.fleet/watch.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
